COREI Institutional Vocabulary
Why this document is important

One of the biggest reasons large systems fail after 5–10 years is that every engineer starts using different words for the same thing.

For example:

Engineer A says:

Dashboard

Engineer B says:

Workspace

Engineer C says:

View

Engineer D says:

Screen

Engineer E says:

Page

Suddenly the architecture becomes inconsistent.

The same thing happens with:

Module
Service
Engine
Runtime
Component
Widget
Plugin
Extension
Tool

Nobody knows the exact meaning anymore.

Institutions avoid this by defining a controlled vocabulary.

Example
❌ BAD

Dashboard

What does dashboard mean?

Nobody knows.

✅ GOOD

Workspace

Definition:

A governed operational environment composed from Institutional Capabilities for achieving one professional operational objective.

There is only one meaning.

Another Example

Capability

Not:

React Component.

Definition:

A governed institutional responsibility executed inside the Workbench Runtime and presented through one or more UI Components.

Huge difference.

Another Example

Component

Definition:

A reusable presentation element used to render part of a Capability.

A Component has no institutional responsibility.

Now engineers immediately understand the difference.

Another Example

Workbench

Definition:

The constitutional runtime responsible for hosting, orchestrating and governing Institutional Workspaces.

Not:

Shell.

Not:

Application.

Not:

Dashboard.

Another Example

Operator

Definition:

A professional institutional user responsible for observation, investigation, decision-making, execution or governance.

Not:

User.

Another Example

Workspace

Definition:

A persistent operational environment supporting one institutional objective.

Not:

Page.

Another Example

Capability Group

Definition:

A logical collection of related Institutional Capabilities serving one operational responsibility.

Another Example

Capability

Definition:

The smallest governed operational responsibility reusable across multiple Workspaces.

Another Example

Panel

Definition:

A visual container used to display one Capability.

Panel is visual.

Capability is architectural.

Never mix them.

Another Example

Widget

Definition:

A small reusable visual element contained within a Panel.

Widget is UI.

Capability is institution.

Another Example

Tool

Definition:

An external system integrated into COREI.

Examples:

Kubernetes
Grafana
Vault
PostgreSQL
TradingView
Kafka

COREI does not replace these.

COREI orchestrates them.

This matches everything we've discussed over the last several days.

Another Example

Engine

Definition:

A deterministic processing subsystem responsible for producing institutional outcomes.

Examples:

Risk Engine
Decision Engine
Execution Engine
Another Example

Service

Definition:

A runtime subsystem providing shared institutional functionality.

Examples:

Context Service
Search Service
Notification Service
Another Example

Runtime

Definition:

The execution environment responsible for lifecycle management, orchestration, communication and governance.

Another Example

Extension

Definition:

A governed package that introduces additional Capabilities into the Workbench without modifying the constitutional architecture.

Another Example

Layout

Definition:

A visual arrangement of Panels.

Nothing more.

Never confuse Layout with Workspace.

Another Example

Workspace Blueprint

Definition:

An implementation-independent specification describing:

objective
capabilities
workflows
context
layout
runtime
persistence
Another Example

Institutional Memory

Definition:

The accumulated operational knowledge of COREI.

Not operator memory.

Institution memory.

